    q: "You have two open findings: (A) a critical CVSS 9.8 vulnerability in a library used only by an internal, air-gapped batch job with no network access, and (B) a medium CVSS 6.1 IDOR on the public-facing customer portal. Which do you fix first, and why?",
    sol: "Fix B first. CVSS severity alone doesn't equal real risk — it assumes a reachable, exploitable context. A's exploitability is near-zero given no network access to trigger it, while B is directly reachable by any authenticated user on internet-facing infrastructure and actively leaks customer data today.",
    exp: "Real prioritization is likelihood x impact in the actual deployed context, not the raw CVSS score in isolation.",
