    q: "A SIEM fires an alert: 'user jdoe failed login 40 times in 2 minutes from 15 different countries, then succeeded.' What does this pattern suggest, and what's your immediate response?",
    sol: "This strongly suggests credential stuffing/a distributed brute-force attempt (many geographically dispersed sources trying the same account rapidly) that ultimately succeeded — likely the attacker had a correct or nearly-correct password from a breached-credential list. Immediate response: force-expire the user's current session(s), require a password reset, and if MFA isn't already enforced on this account, enable it before restoring access.",
    exp: "Geographic dispersion + rapid volume + eventual success is a textbook credential-stuffing signature, distinct from a single confused legitimate user mistyping their password a few times.",
