    q: "An analyst observes a compromised host running PowerShell with obfuscated base64-encoded commands shortly after a phishing email was opened. Which ATT&CK phase does this most likely represent, and what detection would help going forward?",
    sol: "This is likely Execution (running attacker code) following Initial Access (the phishing email) — obfuscated PowerShell is a very common technique (T1059.001) for evading simple signature-based detection while executing a next-stage payload. Detection improvement: log and alert on encoded/obfuscated PowerShell invocation patterns specifically (not just 'PowerShell was used', which is far too noisy), and consider PowerShell Constrained Language Mode / script block logging.",
    exp: "Mapping observed behavior to a specific ATT&CK technique (rather than a vague 'suspicious activity') lets you check whether you actually have a targeted detection for that technique, not just a general sense that something looked off.",
