    sol: "A > B > C. A combines high severity, direct internet exposure, and an existing working exploit — the highest real-world exploitability. B has higher raw CVSS but lower exploitability (internal-only, no known exploit) and lower exposure. C is both lower severity and lower exposure.",
    exp: "Raw CVSS score ranks B above A, but factoring in exposure and known exploitability (which CVSS's 'Exploit Code Maturity' temporal metric is meant to capture) correctly reorders it — patch prioritization should never be CVSS score alone.",
