    q: "A server has SSH (22), an admin web panel (8080, no auth required from certain internal ranges), an old unused FTP service (21) nobody remembers enabling, and HTTPS (443) open. List concrete attack-surface reduction steps.",
    sol: "1) Disable/uninstall the unused FTP service entirely (21) — it serves no purpose and transmits credentials in plaintext if ever used. 2) Add authentication to the admin panel (8080) regardless of network-range restrictions — network-based trust alone is fragile (an attacker on the internal network, or a misconfigured range, bypasses it entirely). 3) Restrict SSH (22) to known admin IP ranges/a VPN and enforce key-based auth. 4) Keep HTTPS (443) as the sole general-purpose exposed port after the above hardening.",
    exp: "Every open, unauthenticated, or forgotten service is a potential entry point regardless of whether it's ever been exploited yet — the FTP service here is a classic 'nobody remembers why it's there' finding.",
