    sol: "Deserializing arbitrary untrusted bytes with native Java serialization can trigger remote code execution via known 'gadget chain' exploits (leveraging classes already on the classpath), regardless of what the intended object shape was. Fix: don't use native object serialization for untrusted client-supplied data at all — replace it with a signed, structured format (e.g. a JWT, or JSON with a strict schema and an HMAC signature verified before any parsing), and if legacy deserialization can't be removed immediately, apply an allowlist-based ObjectInputFilter restricting which classes may be deserialized.",
