    sol: "Length/emptiness validation doesn't address the real risk here — the missing control is output encoding at render time (HTML-entity-encoding < > & etc. so the browser displays the literal text rather than executing it as markup). Validation and encoding solve different problems: validation constrains what data is accepted, encoding makes stored/dynamic data safe for the specific context it's rendered into.",
    exp: "This is a common misconception: passing 'validation' doesn't mean the data is safe to render — the same string is fine as plain text and dangerous as unescaped HTML, which is exactly why encoding has to happen at the point of output, not just at the point of input.",
