    sol: "Add Strict-Transport-Security first if the site already serves HTTPS by default — it's a one-line header (Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains) with essentially no functional risk of breaking anything, and it closes a real gap (a user's first HTTP visit, or a downgrade attempt, without it). X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff is similarly low-risk to add. A meaningful CSP takes more work (auditing every actual script/style source in use) and carries real risk of breaking the site if done carelessly — worth prioritizing, but it should be added last, tested thoroughly (report-only mode first) rather than deployed blind.",
    exp: "Not all security headers carry equal implementation risk — HSTS and nosniff are close to zero-risk additions, while CSP needs careful rollout (report-only mode) to avoid breaking legitimate functionality.",
