    q: "A profile picture upload endpoint checks that the filename ends in .jpg, .png, or .gif before accepting the file, then stores it directly under /public/uploads/{filename} and serves it as a static file. List the remaining risks and fixes.",
    sol: "1) A filename check alone doesn't verify actual file content — an attacker can name a malicious PHP/HTML file 'avatar.jpg' if the server can be tricked into executing it by content-type or extension confusion; verify actual file content/magic bytes match a real image format. 2) Storing under the exact client-supplied filename risks path traversal (../../shell.php) and overwriting another user's file — generate a new random filename/ID server-side instead. 3) Serving uploads from the same static directory as executable code (if misconfigured) risks a web shell — store uploads in a location/bucket with no execute permission and, ideally, a different domain/subdomain than the main app.",
    exp: "Extension checking is trivially bypassed (renaming a file, or exploiting how the server actually determines what to execute) — real validation checks file content, and safe storage removes the ability to execute whatever was uploaded even if validation is somehow bypassed.",
