    q: "A GraphQL API has no query depth or complexity limit, and introspection is enabled in production. Identify the risks and the fixes.",
    sol: "Without a depth/complexity limit, a deeply nested query (e.g. requesting user -> posts -> comments -> author -> posts -> comments... recursively) can force the resolver to do exponentially more work than the request size suggests — a cheap-looking request causing a resource-exhaustion DoS. Introspection enabled in production hands an attacker the full schema (every type, field, and mutation) for free, removing the reconnaissance cost they'd otherwise have to spend. Fixes: add a query depth limiter and/or a cost-analysis library (e.g. graphql-cost-analysis) that rejects overly expensive queries before execution, and disable introspection in production (or restrict it to authenticated internal tooling).",
    exp: "GraphQL's flexibility is exactly what creates this risk — a single endpoint accepting arbitrary query shapes needs its own resource-limiting layer that a fixed set of REST endpoints doesn't.",
