    lvl: "med",
    q: "A server's sshd_config currently allows PasswordAuthentication yes, PermitRootLogin yes, and has no fail2ban or equivalent installed. Harden it.",
    sol: "Set PasswordAuthentication no (require key-based auth only, immune to password brute-forcing/credential stuffing), set PermitRootLogin no (require logging in as a normal user and using sudo for privileged actions, which also gives an audit trail of who actually performed a privileged action), and install fail2ban (or equivalent) to automatically block IPs after repeated failed auth attempts to slow down/deter any residual brute-force attempts.",
    exp: "Each of these three changes closes a distinct weakness — password brute-forceability, an unaccountable shared root login, and unlimited retry attempts — and together they represent the standard SSH hardening baseline most compliance frameworks expect.",
