    sol: "A reverse proxy / API gateway (e.g. Nginx, or a dedicated API gateway product) sitting in front of the microservices: it terminates TLS once at the edge (backends can then communicate over plain HTTP internally, simplifying certificate management), enforces rate limiting centrally before requests reach any backend, and routes requests to the appropriate internal service based on path/host — clients only ever see the single public domain/gateway, never the individual backend services' addresses or internal topology.",
    exp: "Centralizing these cross-cutting concerns (TLS, rate limiting, routing) at a reverse proxy layer avoids reimplementing each of them independently and inconsistently across 5 separate services.",
