    q: "An app trusts the X-Forwarded-For header to determine a client's real IP for rate limiting and IP-based access control, but the load balancer in front of it doesn't strip/validate this header from incoming requests. What's the risk, and the fix?",
    sol: "A client can simply set their own X-Forwarded-For header to an arbitrary IP (e.g. one on an allowlist, or a different value each request) to evade IP-based rate limiting/access control entirely, since the app is trusting client-supplied input as if it were infrastructure-verified truth. Fix: configure the load balancer to strip any client-supplied X-Forwarded-For header and set its own trustworthy value based on the actual observed connection, and configure the application to only trust X-Forwarded-For when it's known to come from that specific, trusted load balancer (not directly from the internet).",
    exp: "X-Forwarded-For is just a regular HTTP header unless something in the trusted path actively overwrites/validates it — trusting it blindly from an untrusted source defeats the entire purpose of using it for security decisions.",
