    sol: "Run an authorized internal audit using a tool like Hashcat against a copy of the hash database with a large, realistic wordlist plus common mutation rules (leetspeak, appended digits), on GPU hardware comparable to what a real attacker might use, within a fixed time budget. Expected outcome: bcrypt's deliberate slowness means even with GPU acceleration, this should recover only a small fraction of hashes — typically the weakest/most common passwords (e.g. 'Password123') — rather than a large percentage, which is exactly bcrypt's design goal; any password recovered this way should prompt a forced reset for that specific account and inform password-policy strength requirements.",
    exp: "The point of this audit isn't to see if cracking is possible at all (it always partially is, for weak passwords) — it's to confirm the hashing algorithm is doing its job by making the cracking rate low and slow, and to catch the specific weak passwords that do fall, before an actual attacker would.",
